\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces  Synthesized posterior predictive distribution for river flow on the $\qopname  \relax o{log}(1+x)$ scale, with $x$ measured in m$^3$/s, at the December 25, 2022 forecast origin under the exAL-M-T1 model. Observed USGS measurements before the origin are shown in green, and held-out USGS verification after the origin is shown in red. The pink ribbon is the pointwise central 95\% predictive interval (2.5th--97.5th percentiles), and the dark line is the predictive mean on the \(\qopname  \relax o{log}(1+x)\) scale. The ten thin traces connect synthesized predictive sample values. The origin line marks \(T\), and the first forecast lead is \(T+1\). Raw retrospective and forecast products are shown for context. Horizontal guides mark discharge references of approximately 182 and 429\nobreakspace  {}m$^3$/s. \relax }}{12}{figure.caption.5}}
\newlabel{fig:synth1}{{3}{12}{Synthesized posterior predictive distribution for river flow on the $\log (1+x)$ scale, with $x$ measured in m$^3$/s, at the December 25, 2022 forecast origin under the exAL-M-T1 model. Observed USGS measurements before the origin are shown in green, and held-out USGS verification after the origin is shown in red. The pink ribbon is the pointwise central 95\% predictive interval (2.5th--97.5th percentiles), and the dark line is the predictive mean on the \(\log (1+x)\) scale. The ten thin traces connect synthesized predictive sample values. The origin line marks \(T\), and the first forecast lead is \(T+1\). Raw retrospective and forecast products are shown for context. Horizontal guides mark discharge references of approximately 182 and 429~m$^3$/s. \relax }{figure.caption.5}{}}
